<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Kruger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Stephanus Johannes Paulus Kruger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Kruger (1825–1904) was president of the South African Republic (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>) from 1883 to 1902. A veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a> and leader of the resistance to British annexation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-boer-war">First Boer War</a>, he dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> politics, refused political rights to the Uitlanders drawn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a> gold rush, and led the republic into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> in 1899. He died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-kruger.jpg" alt="Paul Kruger" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Kruger_Cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephanus Johannes Paulus Kruger was born on 10 October 1825 at Bulhoek, a farm in the Steynsburg area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, the third child of the farmer Casper Jan Hendrik Kruger. As a child he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a>. He had almost no education apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, which he took literally and knew in large part by heart. He became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burgher</a> at sixteen, settled near Rustenburg and married Maria du Plessis in 1842. As field cornet he took part in the Battle of Dimawe against the Tswana chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sechele-i">Sechele I</a> in 1852, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> commando wrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>'s mission at Kolobeng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A protégé of the Voortrekker leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>, Kruger witnessed the signing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sand-river-convention">Sand River Convention</a> in 1852. In 1863 he was elected Commandant-General, a post he held for a decade. He became vice president in March 1877, shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the republic as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, and led two deputations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> against the annexation. He was the leading figure in the movement to restore independence, which ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>' victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-boer-war">First Boer War</a> of 1880 to 1881, and he served in an executive triumvirate until he was elected president in 1883.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1884 Kruger led a third deputation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which secured the London Convention, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the South African Republic as fully independent. After gold was found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a> in 1886, the uitlanders, mostly British, provided almost all tax revenue but had no vote. This question dominated his presidency, which was renewed in 1888, 1893 and 1898, and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895 to 1896 and the Second Boer War.</p><p>He took no part in the fighting, directing the war from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> by telegram. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> on 11 September 1900, was carried from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> to Marseille on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> warship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> and refused to return after the British victory. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1904, and his body was returned for a state funeral in Pretoria. Admirers see him as a tragic folk hero; critics call him a stubborn defender of an unjust cause and an oppressor of black Africans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul Kruger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As tension between <b>Paul Kruger</b> and Britain rose in the late 1890s, President Steyn hosted the Bloemfontein Conference of May–June 1899 between Kruger and the British High Commissioner Alfred Milner, but the talks failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Paul Kruger</b>, president from 1883 until 1902, dominated the later republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Paul Kruger</b> proclaimed the Fountains Valley, at the southern entrance to Pretoria, a nature reserve.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. Died in Clarens, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.44,6.89&amp;year=1876" aria-label="Clarens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4712 530 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Paul Kruger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5162 800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5162 800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.44,6.89&amp;year=1876">What was here in 1876? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
